 "scheme": "ELOT 743",
 "notes": [
  "Romanization: ELOT 743 transliteration of the spelling as printed (polytonic marks reduced to the stress accent, breathings and iota subscripts dropped; monosyllables unaccented except 'í', 'or'); the Greek question mark ';' and raised point '·' are given as '?' and ';'.",
  "line 1.1: 'κόψι' is the cutting edge of the sword, or its cut, its stroke.",
  "line 1.3: 'ὄψι' is the look, face or appearance.",
  "line 3.1: 'In there': in the bones of the Greeks (stanza 2), where Liberty dwelt while Greece was enslaved.",
  "line 4.3: 'σκιάζω' (Heptanesian) is 'to frighten, cow'; 'φοβέρα' is threat or intimidation.",
  "line 6.1: 'ἀκαρτέρει' is read as continuing the second person of stanza 3 ('[you] waited and waited'); grammatically it can also be a third person, with 'a liberty-loving voice' as the one waiting. Unsure.",
  "line 7.2: 'ἐρμιές' are desolate, lonely places (Heptanesian 'ερημιές'); 'τσ'' is the Heptanesian article 'τις'.",
  "line 10.4: 'κουρταλῇ' (Heptanesian) = knocks.",
  "line 13.1: the foot is Liberty's: she turns back and treads swiftly on any stone or grass that reminds her of her former glory.",
  "line 14.3: 'θυροδέρνει' = goes begging from door to door, literally 'beats on doors'.",
  "line 15.4: 'θανῆ' (Heptanesian) = death.",
  "line 18.3: Rigas Feraios (Velestinlis), the revolutionary poet executed in 1798.",
  "lines 20.1-21.4: the Ionian Islands, then a British protectorate: each is chained 'cunningly' ('τεχνικά', with art) and bears 'False Liberty' on its brow.",
  "line 23.1: the lion on a tower: the arms of Castile and León; Spain was then in revolution (1820-1823).",
  "line 24.1: 'Ἐλαφιάσθη' = was startled, shied (from 'ἐλάφι', deer); line 24.2 'σέρνει' = drags, here sends forth."
 ]
